-- Course 1: Workplace Health & Safety in UK Retail
VALUES ('c1000001-aabb-bbcc-ccdd-000000000001', 'Workplace Health & Safety in UK Retail', 'A comprehensive 30-minute course aligned with the Health and Safety at Work Act 1974, HSE guidance, and UK retail industry standards. Covers legal duties, hazard identification, risk assessment, manual handling, fire safety, accident reporting, and RIDDOR obligations. Suitable for all retail staff as part of mandatory induction training.', 'step_by_step', 30, 'a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890', 'published', true, 70);



('b1010002-0001-0001-0001-000000000002', 'a1000001-0001-0001-0001-000000000001', 'Employer and Employee Responsibilities', 2, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Roles and Responsibilities"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Effective health and safety in retail requires a shared commitment between employers and employees. Both have distinct legal obligations under the HSWA and supporting regulations."},{"type":"subheading","content":"Employer Responsibilities"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Provide and maintain a safe working environment, including safe access and egress","Carry out suitable and sufficient risk assessments and act on the findings","Provide adequate information, instruction, training, and supervision","Appoint one or more competent persons to assist with health and safety duties","Consult employees on health and safety matters (Safety Representatives and Safety Committees Regulations 1977)","Report certain workplace injuries, diseases, and dangerous occurrences under RIDDOR 2013","Maintain employers liability insurance (minimum £5 million cover)"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Employee Responsibilities"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Take reasonable care for your own health and safety and that of others","Cooperate with your employer on all health and safety matters","Use work equipment, PPE, and substances in accordance with training","Report any hazard, defect, or near-miss to your supervisor or manager immediately","Do not misuse or interfere with anything provided for health and safety purposes","Attend mandatory health and safety training and follow safe working procedures"]},{"type":"infobox","content":"Under the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, employees must inform their employer of any situation they consider a serious and immediate danger."},{"type":"highlight","content":"Health and safety is a shared legal responsibility. Both employers and employees can face prosecution for failing to meet their duties under the HSWA."}]'::jsonb),

('b1020001-0001-0002-0001-000000000001', 'a1000002-0001-0002-0001-000000000001', 'Common Retail Hazards', 3, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Identifying Hazards in the Retail Environment"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"A hazard is anything with the potential to cause harm. In retail, hazards vary from physical dangers on the shop floor to ergonomic risks in stockrooms."},{"type":"image","url":"/elearning/hs-risk-assessment.jpg","content":"Risk assessment being conducted in a retail store"},{"type":"subheading","content":"Common Retail Hazards"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Slips, trips, and falls — wet floors, uneven surfaces, trailing cables, stock left in walkways (over 30% of all reported retail injuries — HSE)","Manual handling — lifting, carrying, pushing heavy stock without proper technique","Working at height — using ladders or step-stools to access shelving","Display and racking hazards — overloaded or unstable shelving","Workplace violence — verbal abuse, threatening behaviour from customers","Electrical hazards — faulty equipment, damaged cables, overloaded sockets","Hazardous substances — cleaning chemicals, fuel on forecourt sites","Vehicle movements — delivery vehicles in loading bays, customer cars on forecourts"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"The Hierarchy of Controls"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Once a hazard is identified, controls must be applied following the hierarchy (most to least effective):"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. Elimination — remove the hazard entirely","2. Substitution — replace with something less hazardous","3. Engineering controls — physically isolate people from the hazard","4. Administrative controls — change work procedures","5. PPE — personal protective equipment as a last resort"]},{"type":"note","content":"Never walk past a hazard. If you see something unsafe, act on it immediately or report it to your manager."}]'::jsonb),

('b1020002-0001-0002-0001-000000000002', 'a1000002-0001-0002-0001-000000000001', 'The 5-Step Risk Assessment Process', 4, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"HSE 5-Step Risk Assessment"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Under the Management of Health and Safety at Work Regulations 1999, every employer must carry out a suitable and sufficient assessment of risks. If you have 5 or more employees, findings must be recorded in writing."},{"type":"infobox","content":"A risk assessment is not about creating huge amounts of paperwork. It is about identifying sensible measures to control the risks in your workplace."},{"type":"subheading","content":"The 5 Steps"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Step 1: Identify the hazards — Walk around your workplace, consult employees, check manufacturer instructions, review accident records.","Step 2: Decide who might be harmed and how — Consider employees, customers, visitors, contractors, young workers, new starters, pregnant workers.","Step 3: Evaluate the risks and decide on control measures — Assess likelihood and severity. Apply the hierarchy of controls.","Step 4: Record your findings and implement them — Document hazards, who is at risk, existing controls, and further actions needed.","Step 5: Review and update — Review regularly, and whenever there is a significant change or after an accident."]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Risk Rating Matrix"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Risk = Likelihood × Severity"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Low risk (1–4): Acceptable — monitor and review","Medium risk (5–9): Reduce risk further where reasonably practicable","High risk (10–16): Immediate action required","Critical risk (17–25): Stop work immediately"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"Risk assessments are living documents. They must be reviewed regularly and updated whenever circumstances change or after any incident."}]'::jsonb),

('b1030001-0001-0003-0001-000000000001', 'a1000003-0001-0003-0001-000000000001', 'Fire Safety & Emergency Procedures', 5, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Fire Safety in UK Retail Premises"},{"type":"image","url":"/elearning/hs-fire-safety.jpg","content":"Fire extinguisher and emergency exit sign in a retail environment"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 places a duty on the responsible person to carry out a fire risk assessment, implement fire safety measures, and ensure all staff receive adequate fire safety training."},{"type":"subheading","content":"Fire Prevention Measures"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Keep all fire exits and escape routes clear and unlocked during working hours","Store flammable materials safely and away from ignition sources","Maintain electrical equipment — PAT test portable appliances, never overload sockets","Practice good housekeeping — remove waste and combustible materials regularly","No smoking in enclosed workplaces (Health Act 2006)","Report any fire safety defects immediately"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Emergency Evacuation Procedure"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. On hearing the fire alarm, stop what you are doing immediately","2. Do NOT stop to collect personal belongings","3. Leave by the nearest safe exit — assist customers and colleagues","4. Do NOT use lifts during an evacuation","5. Close doors behind you to slow fire and smoke","6. Go directly to the designated assembly point","7. Report to the fire marshal for a roll call — do not re-enter"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"PASS Technique for Fire Extinguishers"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["P — Pull the pin","A — Aim at the base of the fire","S — Squeeze the handle","S — Sweep from side to side"]},{"type":"infobox","content":"UK law requires a minimum of 2 fire drills per year. You must know the location of all fire exits, extinguishers, alarm call points, and your assembly point."},{"type":"note","content":"If in doubt — GET OUT, STAY OUT, and CALL 999."}]'::jsonb),

('b1030002-0001-0003-0001-000000000002', 'a1000003-0001-0003-0001-000000000001', 'Accident Reporting, First Aid & RIDDOR', 6, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Accident Reporting & First Aid Obligations"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"All workplace accidents, incidents, and near-misses must be recorded. Certain incidents must also be reported to the HSE under RIDDOR 2013."},{"type":"subheading","content":"The Accident Book (BI 510)"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Every employer must keep an accident book (BI 510 format, GDPR compliant)","All workplace injuries, however minor, must be recorded","Records must include: date, time, location, injury details, how it happened, treatment given","Accident records must be kept for a minimum of 3 years","The accident book must be readily accessible to all employees"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"RIDDOR Reporting"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The employer must report the following to the HSE:"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Deaths — reported immediately by telephone and written report within 10 days","Specified injuries — fractures, amputations, loss of sight, crush injuries, burns requiring hospital admission","Over-7-day incapacitation — worker unable to perform normal duties for 7+ consecutive days","Non-fatal injuries to non-workers — customer/visitor taken directly to hospital","Occupational diseases — carpal tunnel, dermatitis, hand-arm vibration syndrome","Dangerous occurrences — near-misses such as structural collapse, electrical incidents"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"First Aid Requirements"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Employers must provide adequate first aid equipment, facilities, and personnel","A suitable first aid kit must be available — contents based on risk assessment","At least one appointed person must be designated for first aid arrangements","For higher-risk environments, trained First Aiders at Work (FAW) should be on-site","First aid qualifications must be revalidated every 3 years"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"Never move a seriously injured person unless they are in immediate danger. Call 999 for life-threatening injuries."},{"type":"note","content":"Congratulations on completing this course! Complete the assessment to earn your certificate."}]'::jsonb);

-- Course 1 Assessment


-- Course 2: Food Safety & Hygiene Level 2
VALUES ('c2000001-aabb-bbcc-ccdd-000000000002', 'Food Safety & Hygiene Level 2', 'A comprehensive 30-minute course aligned with the Food Safety Act 1990, EC Regulation 852/2004, and FSA guidelines. Covers food safety law, personal hygiene, food contamination, allergen management, temperature control, cleaning schedules, and HACCP principles. Equivalent to the nationally recognised Level 2 Award in Food Safety in Retail.', 'classic', 30, 'c3d4e5f6-a7b8-9012-cdef-123456789012', 'published', true, 80);



('b2010002-0002-0001-0001-000000000002', 'a2000001-0002-0001-0002-000000000001', 'Personal Hygiene for Food Handlers', 2, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Personal Hygiene Standards"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Poor personal hygiene is one of the leading causes of food contamination. Every food handler must maintain the highest standards of cleanliness."},{"type":"subheading","content":"Handwashing — The Most Important Control"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Hands are the single most common vehicle for transferring bacteria to food. You must wash your hands:"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Before handling any food","After handling raw meat, poultry, fish, or eggs","After using the toilet","After touching your face, hair, or nose","After handling waste or cleaning chemicals","After handling money","After coughing, sneezing, or blowing your nose","When entering the food preparation area"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Correct Handwashing Technique (20 seconds minimum)"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. Wet hands with warm running water","2. Apply antibacterial soap","3. Rub palms together vigorously","4. Wash backs of hands, between fingers, and around thumbs","5. Clean under fingernails","6. Rinse thoroughly under running water","7. Dry with a single-use paper towel — never use a cloth towel"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Appearance and Clothing"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Wear clean protective clothing appropriate to your role","Remove or cover all jewellery (except a plain wedding band)","Keep fingernails short, clean, free from nail varnish","Tie back long hair and cover with a hat or hairnet","Do not wear strong perfume in food areas"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Illness Reporting"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Diarrhoea and/or vomiting","Skin infections, sores, or cuts that cannot be covered","Ear, eye, or nose discharge","Jaundice"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"You must NOT handle food for a minimum of 48 hours after your last episode of diarrhoea or vomiting (FSA/NHS requirement)."},{"type":"note","content":"Blue plasters are used in food environments because blue is not a natural food colour, making them easier to spot if they fall into food."}]'::jsonb),

('b2020001-0002-0002-0001-000000000001', 'a2000002-0002-0002-0002-000000000001', 'Types of Food Contamination', 3, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Understanding Food Contamination"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Food contamination occurs when food is made unsafe by the presence of harmful substances. There are four main types."},{"type":"subheading","content":"1. Biological Contamination"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"The most common and dangerous type:"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Bacteria — Salmonella, E. coli O157, Campylobacter, Listeria, Staphylococcus aureus, Clostridium perfringens, Bacillus cereus","Viruses — Norovirus (most common cause of foodborne illness in the UK), Hepatitis A","Parasites — Cryptosporidium, Toxoplasma","Moulds and yeasts — can produce harmful mycotoxins"]},{"type":"infobox","content":"The FSA estimates approximately 2.4 million cases of foodborne illness in the UK each year, resulting in around 16,000 hospitalisations."},{"type":"subheading","content":"2. Chemical Contamination"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Cleaning chemicals stored near or mixed with food","Pesticide residues on fruit and vegetables","Chemicals leaching from damaged food containers","Excessive use of food additives"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"3. Physical Contamination"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Glass fragments from broken bottles or light fittings","Metal fragments from worn equipment","Hair, plasters, jewellery, or fingernails","Insects or pest droppings","Plastic or packaging material"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"4. Allergenic Contamination (Cross-Contact)"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Occurs when an allergen is unintentionally transferred to food that should not contain it. Even trace amounts can trigger anaphylaxis."},{"type":"highlight","content":"Cross-contamination is the transfer of harmful organisms from one food, surface, or person to another. Always separate raw and cooked foods."}]'::jsonb),

('b2020002-0002-0002-0001-000000000002', 'a2000002-0002-0002-0002-000000000001', 'Allergen Management & Natasha''s Law', 4, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"The 14 Major Food Allergens"},{"type":"image","url":"/elearning/fs-allergens.jpg","content":"Food allergen labelling on product packaging"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Under UK food law, 14 allergens must be declared when used as ingredients. Businesses must provide accurate allergen information at the point of sale."},{"type":"subheading","content":"The 14 Allergens"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. Celery","2. Cereals containing gluten (wheat, rye, barley, oats)","3. Crustaceans (prawns, crab, lobster)","4. Eggs","5. Fish","6. Lupin","7. Milk (including lactose)","8. Molluscs (mussels, oysters, squid)","9. Mustard","10. Tree nuts (almonds, hazelnuts, walnuts, cashews, pecans, Brazils, pistachios, macadamia)","11. Peanuts","12. Sesame","13. Soybeans","14. Sulphur dioxide and sulphites (above 10mg/kg)"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Natasha''s Law (October 2021)"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Named after Natasha Ednan-Laperouse, who died from an allergic reaction. Requires all prepacked for direct sale (PPDS) food to carry a full ingredients list with allergens in bold."},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["PPDS includes food packaged at the same premises from which it is sold","Full ingredients list must be displayed on packaging","All 14 allergens must be emphasised in bold","Food business name and address must appear on label"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Preventing Cross-Contact"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Store allergen-containing ingredients separately","Use separate utensils and surfaces for allergen-free food","Wash hands between handling different allergens","Clean all equipment between preparing different foods","Never guess — check the label or tell the customer you cannot confirm"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"A food allergy can be fatal. Never downplay a customer''s allergy enquiry. If unsure, say so — do not guess."}]'::jsonb),

('b2030001-0002-0003-0001-000000000001', 'a2000003-0002-0003-0002-000000000001', 'Temperature Control & Food Storage', 5, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Temperature Control Requirements"},{"type":"image","url":"/elearning/fs-temperature.jpg","content":"Checking food temperatures using a digital probe thermometer"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Temperature control is one of the most critical aspects of food safety. Bacteria multiply most rapidly in the Danger Zone — between 8°C and 63°C."},{"type":"subheading","content":"Key Temperatures"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Chilled storage: 0°C to 5°C (legal max 8°C — best practice 5°C or below)","Frozen storage: -18°C or below","Hot holding: 63°C or above","Cooking: core 75°C for at least 2 minutes","Reheating (Scotland): 82°C minimum","Reheating (England/Wales/NI): 75°C recommended","Cooling: 63°C to below 8°C within 90 minutes"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"The 2-Hour / 4-Hour Rule"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Under 2 hours out of fridge: can be returned","Between 2 and 4 hours: use immediately or discard","Over 4 hours: MUST be discarded"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Temperature Monitoring"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Check fridge/freezer temperatures at least twice daily","Use a calibrated digital probe thermometer","Reject deliveries above 8°C (chilled) or above -15°C (frozen)","Clean probes between uses","Keep logs for minimum 12 months"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Storage Rules"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Raw food below ready-to-eat food","FIFO — First In, First Out","Use-by dates: criminal offence to sell past this date","Keep food covered and labelled","Never re-freeze defrosted food unless cooked first"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"It is a criminal offence to sell food past its use-by date."}]'::jsonb),

('b2030002-0002-0003-0001-000000000002', 'a2000003-0002-0003-0002-000000000001', 'Cleaning, Disinfection & HACCP Principles', 6, 5, '[{"type":"heading","content":"Cleaning and Disinfection"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Effective cleaning removes food residues and bacteria from surfaces and equipment."},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["Cleaning — removes visible dirt (detergent + water)","Disinfection — reduces bacteria to safe level (chemicals or heat)","Sanitising — cleaning AND disinfection combined"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"The 6-Stage Cleaning Process"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. Pre-clean — remove loose debris","2. Main clean — wash with hot water and detergent","3. Rinse — remove detergent with clean water","4. Disinfect — apply disinfectant or use water at 82°C+","5. Final rinse — remove chemical residue if required","6. Air dry — dry naturally, never use cloth towels"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"Cleaning Schedules"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"Every food business must have a documented cleaning schedule:"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["What is to be cleaned","How (method and chemicals)","How often (frequency)","Who is responsible","Safety precautions needed"]},{"type":"subheading","content":"HACCP — Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points"},{"type":"paragraph","content":"A systematic approach to identifying and controlling food safety hazards. Legally required for all UK food businesses."},{"type":"infobox","content":"Safer Food, Better Business (SFBB) is the FSA''s simplified HACCP system for small and medium food businesses."},{"type":"subheading","content":"The 7 HACCP Principles"},{"type":"bullet_points","items":["1. Conduct a hazard analysis","2. Determine Critical Control Points (CCPs)","3. Establish critical limits (e.g., 75°C cooking temp)","4. Establish monitoring procedures","5. Establish corrective actions","6. Establish verification procedures","7. Establish documentation and record keeping"]},{"type":"highlight","content":"HACCP is a legal requirement under EC Regulation 852/2004. Your food business must implement and maintain HACCP-based procedures."},{"type":"note","content":"Congratulations on completing this course! Complete the assessment to earn your certificate."}]'::jsonb);

-- Course 2 Assessment

